00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:13.180
<v Guest>and high enough sort of quality matching the intended problem in order to mitigate something

208
00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:16.640
<v Guest>unintended, but really harmful in many cases, consequences.

209
00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:23.360
<v Noah Labhart>Well, in the book, you mentioned, you know, that business leaders may not have the right

210
00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:24.960
<v Noah Labhart>org structure to work with AI.

211
00:14:25.100 --> 00:14:26.340
<v Noah Labhart>I think it's in chapter five.

212
00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:30.300
<v Noah Labhart>What structures indicate AI readiness for a business?

213
00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:31.560
<v Noah Labhart>How do they know they're ready?

214
00:14:31.940 --> 00:14:34.420
<v Guest>I think there's a lot of, there's many different structures.

215
00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:40.360
<v Guest>some different options that might make sense for different stages of organizations. But the

216
00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:45.360
<v Guest>takeaway is that machine learning and launching AI-based products, it's a team sport.

217
00:14:45.980 --> 00:14:51.260
<v Guest>You cannot do it alone. You can't do it sequestered in one part of an organization.

218
00:14:51.340 --> 00:14:57.860
<v Guest>It really takes the collaboration of people with different types of skill sets, data science or

219
00:14:57.860 --> 00:15:02.700
<v Guest>machine learning training being one of those skill sets, but certainly not the only one.

220
00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:17.380
<v Guest>And I think that's one of the big challenges that we see in organizations is that, you know, there's sort of this idea that you just hire some really smart people and you put them in a basement and, you know, out comes this magical model if you, you know, sort of given them enough time and compute power and data.

221
00:15:17.500 --> 00:15:20.020
<v Guest>And that's really not how it works in successful companies.

222
00:15:20.020 --> 00:15:39.620
<v Guest>Most companies that are good at this and are good at prototyping and getting things sort of actually into production, solving a real problem, have a variety of skill sets on their team from designers and product managers and DevOps engineers and, you know, software, regular software engineers and all sorts of sort of diverse inputs.

223
00:15:39.620 --> 00:15:44.120
<v Guest>that is really a partnership between the business stakeholders

224
00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:46.800
<v Guest>and the technical experts and leadership.

225
00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:52.100
<v Guest>And it's that collaboration that really sort of is where the magic happens

226
00:15:52.100 --> 00:15:56.200
<v Guest>and you need everyone's skill sets in order to be really successful.

227
00:15:56.420 --> 00:15:58.140
<v Guest>And we walk through some different structures

228
00:15:58.140 --> 00:16:01.220
<v Guest>that might work for different sized organizations.

229
00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:07.020
<v Guest>The takeaway is that they all accomplish this collaborative way of working

230
00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:13.800
<v Guest>That's so critical for any project, but more pointedly so for machine learning based products.

231
00:16:14.840 --> 00:16:27.160
<v Noah Labhart>That makes sense. Well, what industries do you think could benefit most from AI and machine learning? Is there one world that's better than the other? Or can everybody do better having this sort of technology?

232
00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:31.240
<v Guest>You know, Wilson, I don't know if you'll agree with me, but I think it's everyone.

233
00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:36.200
<v Guest>I think there's a lot of focus on which industries are better or worse or more ready.

234
00:16:36.340 --> 00:16:46.900
<v Guest>And the short of it is there's opportunity in every single industry for maturation and innovation using this technology.

235
00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:53.620
<v Guest>But actually, in many cases, if you don't need to use AI to solve your problem, you shouldn't.

236
00:16:54.160 --> 00:16:56.900
<v Guest>Because it's a really hard, it's often very expensive.

237
00:16:57.160 --> 00:17:02.020
<v Guest>it's cumbersome, it's challenging, and there's lots of other technologies. I like to tell people

238
00:17:02.020 --> 00:17:07.000
<v Guest>that if you don't absolutely have to use machine learning-based technology, don't. If you can sort

239
00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:13.580
<v Guest>of solve 80% without, do. But the reality is that there's very real difficult problems that are very

240
00:17:13.580 --> 00:17:20.260
<v Guest>well-suited for machine learning-based solutions in every industry. Anytime you're dealing with

241
00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:26.960
<v Guest>with large amounts of data, which is basically every industry, and repetitive tasks, it's

242
00:17:26.960 --> 00:17:28.800
<v Guest>super well suited for machine learning.

243
00:17:29.980 --> 00:17:36.600
<v Guest>I also affirm the believer that AI can really transform all kinds of industries, although

244
00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:39.220
<v Guest>the material level from different industries might be different.

245
00:17:39.580 --> 00:17:45.560
<v Guest>For the high tech industries, and people are using AI, using machine learning for a long

246
00:17:45.560 --> 00:17:47.120
<v Guest>time, 10 years, 20 years there.

247
00:17:47.120 --> 00:17:50.520
<v Guest>They also have a lot of data accumulated.

248
00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:53.520
<v Guest>They track all those user behaviors from the web.

249
00:17:53.740 --> 00:18:00.720
<v Guest>With all this data, they can really leverage AI to do a lot of use cases like recommendations,

250
00:18:01.300 --> 00:18:03.440
<v Guest>like advertisements, a lot of those, right?

251
00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:10.960
<v Guest>Meanwhile, other industries like finance or maybe retail, we see a lot of AI use cases

252
00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:14.760
<v Guest>now and many other industries are also catching up.

253
00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:22.080
<v Guest>Basically, I think any industry where they have a lot of data, there's a lot of potential for leveraging AI to really transform that industry.

254
00:18:23.540 --> 00:18:26.580
<v Noah Labhart>I want to double back a little bit to an earlier chapter.

