00:18:38.060 --> 00:18:38.560
<v Guest>Yeah.

259
00:18:38.540 --> 00:18:42.340
<v Guest>Yeah, so if you're not familiar with the Goldilocks story, you know, classic children's book,

260
00:18:42.500 --> 00:18:46.260
<v Guest>which talks about, you know, something not too hot, not too cold, but just right.

261
00:18:46.920 --> 00:18:51.820
<v Guest>This is one of the things that organizations who are newer to using AI, it's important

262
00:18:51.820 --> 00:18:56.780
<v Guest>to start with a project that is going to be successful, that, you know, sort of is set

263
00:18:56.780 --> 00:18:58.020
<v Guest>up for success from the beginning.

264
00:18:58.120 --> 00:19:00.880
<v Guest>And often that starts with choosing the right problem.

265
00:19:01.060 --> 00:19:06.760
<v Guest>So we talk through some different qualities of what makes a good problem to start with

266
00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:12.680
<v Guest>new in developing that muscle of how to build machine learning based products.

267
00:19:13.060 --> 00:19:19.040
<v Guest>So you don't want an enormous problem that's so vague and so ill-defined and so big that's

268
00:19:19.040 --> 00:19:19.780
<v Guest>hard to make progress.

269
00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:24.360
<v Guest>And you don't want a really small problem that no one's really going to care about if

270
00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:24.860
<v Guest>you solve it.

271
00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:28.920
<v Guest>So you need sort of something that's just right in the middle, that's a big enough problem

