00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:35.940
<v Guest>to solve, that matters if you solve it, that's sort of narrow enough that you can start with,

273
00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:43.520
<v Guest>but broad enough to have impact, that has enough data, that it's not impossible to get the right quality data for,

274
00:19:44.260 --> 00:19:51.640
<v Guest>but also is broad enough that it could be broadened later to solve a bigger and bigger problem as you invest further.

275
00:19:52.040 --> 00:19:58.840
<v Guest>And so we go through some different qualities that help people choose what is a good problem to start with

276
00:19:58.840 --> 00:20:00.860
<v Guest>that's sort of just the right temperature.

277
00:20:01.620 --> 00:20:04.080
<v Guest>In our book, we also shared quite a few examples.

278
00:20:05.980 --> 00:20:13.200
<v Guest>organization I tried to tackle a super noble and also ambitious problem, cure cancer after

279
00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:16.760
<v Guest>spent millions of dollars in the product field, right?

280
00:20:16.880 --> 00:20:19.120
<v Guest>Because that problem is just too big, too broad.

281
00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:23.740
<v Guest>On the other hand, we also shared another story from Autodesk, where when they start

