00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:57.300
<v Guest>they have a lot for historical chat

309
00:21:59.260 --> 00:22:00.040
<v Guest>to password reset.

310
00:22:00.320 --> 00:22:02.940
<v Guest>So they can clean up and label all this data

311
00:22:02.940 --> 00:22:04.740
<v Guest>to really train their AI applications.

312
00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:09.440
<v Noah Labhart>So your story about the Tesla Model 3

313
00:22:09.440 --> 00:22:12.920
<v Noah Labhart>is a great example of what can happen with immature AI,

314
00:22:13.040 --> 00:22:14.560
<v Noah Labhart>obviously Elon's company.

315
00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:18.120
<v Noah Labhart>Can you describe that scenario and how it can be avoided?

316
00:22:19.380 --> 00:22:21.220
<v Guest>Tesla is an incredible company

317
00:22:21.220 --> 00:22:23.380
<v Guest>that is making a huge amount of progress

318
00:22:23.380 --> 00:22:25.900
<v Guest>in self-driving cars, and it's really inspiring,

319
00:22:26.080 --> 00:22:27.700
<v Guest>but it's a hard problem.

320
00:22:29.260 --> 00:22:32.480
<v Guest>in self-driving industries, getting enough data.

321
00:22:32.860 --> 00:22:34.500
<v Guest>And Andrej Karpathy actually,

322
00:22:34.580 --> 00:22:37.360
<v Guest>who's one of the AI experts at Tesla,

323
00:22:37.600 --> 00:22:39.720
<v Guest>he did a talk several years ago

324
00:22:40.160 --> 00:22:43.280
<v Guest>where he said that when he was in academia,

325
00:22:43.620 --> 00:22:46.800
<v Guest>there was sort of 70, 80% of his time

326
00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:48.120
<v Guest>that was spent on model development

327
00:22:48.120 --> 00:22:51.340
<v Guest>and about 20% of time spent on training data.

