00:24:48.050 --> 00:24:49.920
<v Guest>Because that just doesn't happen.

349
00:24:49.920 --> 00:24:54.600
<v Guest>And Tesla has thousands of hundreds of thousands of miles driven and data collected.

350
00:24:54.800 --> 00:24:59.720
<v Guest>But most of that is cars driving normally successfully down a freeway.

351
00:25:00.140 --> 00:25:06.000
<v Noah Labhart>So in the book, you urge people to consider three areas before deploying new AI.

352
00:25:06.220 --> 00:25:08.760
<v Noah Labhart>And then you call them availability, performance and scalability.

353
00:25:09.020 --> 00:25:15.020
<v Noah Labhart>When I when I think about this, I think about, you know, the project management triangle, time, cost and scope.

354
00:25:15.020 --> 00:25:20.460
<v Noah Labhart>So can a business do two out of three of these, or do they have to do all three of them?

355
00:25:20.520 --> 00:25:21.920
<v Noah Labhart>Are there trade-offs between them?

356
00:25:21.960 --> 00:25:22.960
<v Noah Labhart>Can you talk about that a little bit?

357
00:25:24.560 --> 00:25:33.880
<v Guest>Yeah, I think those are the three very important principles for building high-scalable, high-performing systems.

358
00:25:34.340 --> 00:25:38.080
<v Guest>If you ask any engineer, those are not new concepts.

359
00:25:38.500 --> 00:25:44.300
<v Guest>But when people start to build AI, some of those actually got ignored and which caused some problems.

360
00:25:45.900 --> 00:25:53.280
<v Guest>So for those three principles, availability, performance, scalability, availability really

361
00:25:53.280 --> 00:25:59.360
<v Guest>refers to if a lot of people are using your AI product, the product should be available

362
00:25:59.360 --> 00:26:00.240
<v Guest>all the time.

363
00:26:00.440 --> 00:26:06.360
<v Guest>You should not get a, well, I want to use your AI model, you've got an outage, the AI

364
00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:12.240
<v Guest>is not working, then that's not good given just AI is used in so many different scenarios

365
00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:14.580
<v Guest>now in our everyday life.

366
00:26:16.300 --> 00:26:20.400
<v Guest>Performance is more referred to when you interact with AI,

367
00:26:20.800 --> 00:26:22.620
<v Guest>you always get quick response.

368
00:26:23.520 --> 00:26:25.760
<v Guest>Like sometimes if you ask a question,

369
00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:28.620
<v Guest>the AI can only answer you in one or two minutes.

370
00:26:29.220 --> 00:26:32.140
<v Guest>Probably you will lose the patience to use an AI, right?

371
00:26:32.760 --> 00:26:35.980
<v Guest>Scalability refers to if the traffic,

372
00:26:36.080 --> 00:26:38.560
<v Guest>if your user base increase a lot,

373
00:26:38.560 --> 00:26:44.340
<v Guest>the system can scale to support those increased demand or increase the traffic.

374
00:26:44.940 --> 00:26:52.480
<v Guest>So those are the basic principles when anyone designing any engineering systems.

375
00:26:53.060 --> 00:26:58.920
<v Guest>And it's a little bit different from those different triangle trade-offs for project managers.

376
00:26:59.200 --> 00:27:06.480
<v Guest>Because when you build this AI system, you cannot really just only get one and then trade off the other two.

377
00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:10.940
<v Guest>Availability, I think that's always a key focus.

378
00:27:11.140 --> 00:27:12.380
<v Guest>You should always get it right.

379
00:27:12.640 --> 00:27:18.000
<v Guest>No one wants to use an AI system where you always bump into outage.

380
00:27:18.460 --> 00:27:20.260
<v Guest>Performance is super important.

381
00:27:22.640 --> 00:27:29.000
<v Guest>Basically when you use any internet products, the speed actually means money.

382
00:27:29.380 --> 00:27:34.640
<v Guest>If it's a product which results worse, slowly people lose patience, people will give up

383
00:27:34.640 --> 00:27:35.140
<v Guest>the product.

384
00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:42.420
<v Guest>two I think is a must-have. You cannot make any trade-off. Scalability, so that's an interesting

385
00:27:42.420 --> 00:27:49.340
<v Guest>one. When you start to build your application, it might be okay you do not consider the scalability

386
00:27:49.340 --> 00:27:55.960
<v Guest>in the beginning, but once your product gets attraction, you start to see more users coming,

387
00:27:56.160 --> 00:27:59.580
<v Guest>that's time you have to make the investments to make your systems scalable.

388
00:27:59.580 --> 00:28:00.080
<v Guest>available.

389
00:28:01.800 --> 00:28:03.740
<v Guest>Context is king in AI.

390
00:28:03.880 --> 00:28:05.440
<v Guest>In marketing they say content is king.

391
00:28:05.560 --> 00:28:07.460
<v Guest>In AI, context is king.

392
00:28:07.640 --> 00:28:12.180
<v Guest>It really matters, you know, the specifics of the problem that you're trying to solve

393
00:28:12.180 --> 00:28:15.620
<v Guest>and the environment that you're trying to solve it in and who the people are, the players

394
00:28:15.620 --> 00:28:18.500
<v Guest>and what the stakes are of what you're doing.

395
00:28:18.680 --> 00:28:23.420
<v Guest>And it's impossible to sort of say arbitrarily, oh, this always works or this never works

396
00:28:23.420 --> 00:28:28.980
<v Guest>or, you know, balance and context are always really critical to evaluating any problem.

397
00:28:30.280 --> 00:28:37.940
<v Noah Labhart>You know, one of the last chapters, you talk about building versus buying the technology for AI and for machine learning.

398
00:28:38.080 --> 00:28:45.280
<v Noah Labhart>And there's lots of vendors out there now that are touting they can do machine learning or easy to use.

399
00:28:45.520 --> 00:28:55.240
<v Noah Labhart>What helps someone decide if they should create their own infrastructure solution or go out and use someone's prebuilt solution?

400
00:28:56.020 --> 00:29:01.220
<v Guest>One of the things to understand about successfully deploying machine learning products at scale

401
00:29:01.220 --> 00:29:06.960
<v Guest>is that it requires many different technologies, not just one.

402
00:29:07.160 --> 00:29:12.000
<v Guest>And it requires sort of an orchestration or a symphony of all of these technologies working together.

403
00:29:12.200 --> 00:29:19.940
<v Guest>And so for any piece of this system or, you know, a complex machine learning based application,

404
00:29:19.940 --> 00:29:32.500
<v Guest>There are anywhere from five to 100 different types of technologies, depending on how you define that and different types of infrastructure, hardware, software and sort of everything in between.

405
00:29:33.260 --> 00:29:47.240
